WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.830
Attention is a limited operating resource.

2
00:00:02.830 --> 00:00:13.233
Demanding work depends on the ability to hold information in mind, notice what matters, ignore distractions, and update understanding as new evidence appears.

3
00:00:13.233 --> 00:00:19.811
When attention is fragmented, energy drains faster because the brain has to keep rebuilding context.

4
00:00:19.811 --> 00:00:26.083
This is why a day full of interruptions can feel exhausting even if no single task looks difficult.

5
00:00:26.083 --> 00:00:31.514
Cognitive load is the mental effort required to hold, process, and use information.

6
00:00:31.514 --> 00:00:33.273
Working memory has limits.

7
00:00:33.273 --> 00:00:41.840
Complex systems, unclear requirements, unfamiliar concepts, noisy environments, and frequent interruptions all increase load.

8
00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:50.559
When load is high, people may miss details, make weaker tradeoffs, or postpone decisions because the work feels heavier than expected.

9
00:00:50.559 --> 00:00:53.313
Context switching is a common energy leak.

10
00:00:53.313 --> 00:01:01.497
Moving from a code review to a message thread, then to a meeting, then back to the code review, requires repeated reorientation.

11
00:01:01.497 --> 00:01:07.157
Each switch asks attention to reload the goal, the current state, and the next decision.

12
00:01:07.157 --> 00:01:08.764
The cost is not only time.

13
00:01:08.764 --> 00:01:13.200
It is also mental friction, error risk, and the gradual loss of depth.

14
00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:18.095
Demanding work should be protected from low-energy windows when possible.

15
00:01:18.095 --> 00:01:20.773
That does not mean every day will be ideal.

16
00:01:20.773 --> 00:01:23.985
It means matching task type to attention quality.

17
00:01:23.985 --> 00:01:31.481
Use higher-quality attention for design, troubleshooting, writing, learning, and decisions with consequences.

18
00:01:31.481 --> 00:01:38.594
Use lower-energy windows for routine cleanup, scheduling, simple approvals, and administrative batching.

19
00:01:38.594 --> 00:01:42.725
This makes energy a planning input rather than an afterthought.
